    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **22 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same firstName and lastName, case-insensitively \u2014 following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer property to the `Owner` schema (alongside `customerCode`/`householdId`). This regenerates `OwnerDto` with the field, and MapStruct maps it automatically by name.\n\n2. **`Owner.java` model** \u2014 Added a persisted `namesakeCount` field (`@Column(name = \"namesake_count\")`) with getter/setter, so the value survives to a later `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n3. **Schema SQL** (hsqldb, h2, postgres, mysql) \u2014 Added the `namesake_count INTEGER` column to the `owners` table so the tests (hsqldb) and app (h2) both have it.\n\n4. **New function `AssignNamesakeCount.java`** \u2014 Counts existing owners matching firstName + lastName case-insensitively via `ownerRepository.findAll()`, and sets the count on the owner. It runs *before* `SaveOwner`, so the new owner isn't counted \u2014 matching the sibling `AssignCustomerCode` pattern.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 Wired the new step into the create pipeline (`assignHousehold \u2192 assignNamesakeCount \u2192 save`) with `govern: [ transaction ]` like every other step.\n\nThe new `Cp13Tests.coreCountsNamesakes` passes (owner b gets `namesakeCount = 1` after owner a with the same name is created), and no existing tests regressed.",
